#: ../../../dev/implementation_guidelines.md:3
msgid "Implementation Guidelines"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/implementation_guidelines.md:5
msgid ""
"{ref}`code_style` covers naming, macros, and formatting. This page covers "
"the judgment calls that come up while writing an implementation: what to "
"optimize for, how to use memory, when to check inputs, and how to handle "
"headers. {ref}`patterns_to_avoid` lists the mistakes and outdated patterns "
"that reviewers see most often."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/implementation_guidelines.md:11
msgid "Priorities"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/implementation_guidelines.md:13
msgid "When goals conflict, prefer them in this order:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/implementation_guidelines.md:15
msgid ""
"**Correctness.** Follow the standard. See {ref}`undefined_behavior` for what "
"to do where the standard is silent."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/implementation_guidelines.md:17
msgid ""
"**Readability.** Code that is long but obvious is better than code that is "
"short and clever. The character classification tables in `src/__support/"
"ctype_utils.h` are long, and anyone can see what they do."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/implementation_guidelines.md:20
msgid ""
"**Efficiency.** This encompasses latency, throughput, binary size, and "
"memory usage. There's no consistent formula for tradeoffs between these, but "
"in general baremetal users prioritize binary size and servers prioritize "
"latency and throughput."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/implementation_guidelines.md:25
msgid ""
"The memory functions and the math library are exceptions, where performance "
"is an explicit goal alongside correctness."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/implementation_guidelines.md:28
msgid ""
"Microbenchmarks are useful for finding a direction but are not a goal in and "
"of themselves. The ideal performance measurement is from sampling real "
"systems, though that's impractical for most changes. The project does not "
"have a settled benchmarking setup today. LLVM's [benchmarking tips](https://"
"llvm.org/docs/Benchmarking.html) describe how to take more meaningful "
"measurements."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/implementation_guidelines.md:35
msgid "Memory"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/implementation_guidelines.md:37
msgid ""
"**Allocate on the heap only when necessary.** Many targets have a small heap "
"or none. When allocation is needed, follow the allocation rules in {ref}"
"`code_style` and handle failure."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/implementation_guidelines.md:40
msgid ""
"**Stack buffers may be large when the function needs them.** Prefer a "
"correct implementation with a generous buffer over a complicated one. Sizes "
"can be tuned later."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/implementation_guidelines.md:43
msgid ""
"**Do not use unbounded recursion.** Baremetal users must be able to "
"determine the maximum stack depth statically. Recursion with an explicit "
"depth limit is acceptable."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/implementation_guidelines.md:47
msgid "Allocators"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/implementation_guidelines.md:49
msgid ""
"LLVM-libc does not aim to provide its own general-purpose allocator for "
"hosted systems. On Linux the supported allocator is [Scudo](https://llvm.org/"
"docs/ScudoHardenedAllocator.html), and users with specific performance "
"requirements are expected to bring the allocator they already prefer."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/implementation_guidelines.md:55
msgid ""
"Scudo is not used on baremetal targets. It is designed for hosted systems "
"and gets its memory from the operating system by mapping pages. A baremetal "
"target has no operating system to ask, and usually has a small, fixed region "
"of memory. On baremetal targets LLVM-libc instead provides an optimized "
"allocator in `src/__support/freelist_heap.h`, which manages the memory "
"between two symbols that the linker script provides."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/implementation_guidelines.md:62
msgid "Assembly"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/implementation_guidelines.md:64
msgid ""
"LLVM-libc is written in C++. Assembly is used only in the few places where "
"the stack or individual registers must be controlled exactly, or where an "
"instruction has no compiler builtin. Assembly written for performance is not "
"accepted, because it gives up what the compiler provides for C++ code: "
"diagnostics, sanitizers, coverage and profile instrumentation, and debug "
"information. {ref}`assembly_policy` has the full policy, including how "
"patches that contain assembly are reviewed."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/implementation_guidelines.md:72
msgid "Runtime checks"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/implementation_guidelines.md:74
msgid ""
"**Null pointers.** At the start of an entrypoint, pass each pointer argument "
"that would be dereferenced, and for which a null pointer is not a valid "
"input, to `LIBC_CRASH_ON_NULLPTR` (`src/__support/macros/null_check.h`). The "
"check is lightweight, is controlled by the `LIBC_ADD_NULL_CHECKS` option, "
"and exists to make a crash easy to diagnose. Put it in the entrypoint only, "
"not in internal code. Do not add the check for a pointer that the libc only "
"passes on to the kernel without dereferencing it. The kernel checks such "
"pointers safely and returns `EFAULT` for any invalid address, not only for a "
"null pointer."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/implementation_guidelines.md:83
msgid ""
"**Internal invariants.** Use `LIBC_ASSERT`. It compiles away in release "
"builds."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/implementation_guidelines.md:85
msgid ""
"**Hardening.** `LIBC_COPT_HARDENING_MODE` was added for the allocator's "
"integrity checks. Extending it to other code needs discussion first."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/implementation_guidelines.md:87
msgid ""
"**Everything else.** Do not validate inputs beyond what the standard "
"requires. {ref}`undefined_behavior` explains the reasoning."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/implementation_guidelines.md:90
msgid "Headers"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/implementation_guidelines.md:92
msgid ""
"**Include what you use.** Every name a file uses must be made available by a "
"header that the file includes directly, not by a transitive inclusion. A "
"file that names `mode_t` includes `hdr/types/mode_t.h` itself, even if "
"another header it includes happens to provide the type. This applies to "
"header files as well as `.cpp` files. The exception is a header whose "
"purpose is to include another one, such as a proxy header or a header that "
"selects the implementation for the target: include that header, not the file "
"it forwards to."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/implementation_guidelines.md:100
msgid ""
"**Use proxy headers** from `libc/hdr/` for every type and macro that the "
"libc also provides publicly. See the header inclusion policy in {ref}"
"`code_style`. Compiler-provided headers are tolerated but should also go "
"through a proxy header where one exists. The existing `#include <pthread.h>` "
"style includes under `libc/src/` are incorrect and should not be copied."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/implementation_guidelines.md:106
msgid ""
"**`src/__support/CPP/` headers** are included through the top-level header, "
"for example `src/__support/CPP/type_traits.h` or `src/__support/CPP/"
"utility.h`, not through the individual files in the subdirectories of the "
"same name. The top-level header is a forwarding header of the kind the first "
"point describes."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/implementation_guidelines.md:114
msgid "Feature-test macros in public headers"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/implementation_guidelines.md:116
msgid ""
"Public headers do not use feature-test macros such as `_GNU_SOURCE` or "
"`_POSIX_C_SOURCE` to hide declarations. Do not add `#ifdef` guards of that "
"kind to a header. The exception is a macro that a standard itself defines "
"for this purpose, such as `__STDC_WANT_LIB_EXT1__` for Annex K."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/implementation_guidelines.md:121
msgid ""
"Headers are generated from the YAML files in `libc/include/` (see {ref}"
"`header_generation`), and a generated header declares only the functions "
"that are enabled for the target. A user who wants a different API surface "
"generates headers to match it, by pointing the `LIBC_CONFIG_PATH` CMake "
"option at their own `entrypoints.txt`, `headers.txt`, and configuration (see "
"{ref}`custom_config_directory`), instead of every user receiving one set of "
"headers full of conditionals."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/implementation_guidelines.md:129
msgid ""
"Each function in the YAML files lists the `standards` it comes from (`stdc`, "
"`posix`, `gnu`, `bsd`, `linux`, `llvm_libc_ext`, and so on). Keep that field "
"accurate. It records where each declaration comes from."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/implementation_guidelines.md:133
msgid "{ref}`writing_public_headers` has the other rules for public headers."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/implementation_guidelines.md:135
msgid "File headers and comments"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/implementation_guidelines.md:137
msgid ""
"Every file touched by a change uses the current LLVM file header: a bare "
"separator line, the license block, and a `\\file` description."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/implementation_guidelines.md:155
msgid ""
"Comment only what the code does not already say. A straightforward function "
"or test needs no introductory paragraph, and a function that is moved keeps "
"exactly the comments it had. Also, don't number the steps of a function in "
"its comments (`// 1. ...`, `// 2. ...`). It makes later refactors noisier "
"than necessary for no tangible benefit."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/implementation_guidelines.md:161
msgid "Character data"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/implementation_guidelines.md:163
msgid ""
"Do not compare against character literals (except for equality), or do "
"arithmetic on them to classify or convert characters. Use the helpers in "
"`src/__support/ctype_utils.h` and `src/__support/wctype_utils.h`, which work "
"for non-ASCII encodings and for both `char` and `wchar_t`. See {ref}"
"`design_philosophy` for the model behind this."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/implementation_guidelines.md:171
msgid "Math functions"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/implementation_guidelines.md:173
msgid ""
"The {ref}`math page <math>` describes the goals of the math library, and "
"`libc/src/math/docs/add_math_function.md` is the step-by-step guide for "
"adding a function. The points below are the ones that most often need "
"stating."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/implementation_guidelines.md:177
msgid "Accuracy and error reporting"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/implementation_guidelines.md:179
msgid ""
"The default implementation of every math function is expected to be "
"correctly rounded in all rounding modes, and to support `errno`, floating "
"point exceptions, and signaling NaNs. A signaling NaN input produces a quiet "
"NaN and raises `FE_INVALID`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/implementation_guidelines.md:183
msgid "The exceptions to that expectation are:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/implementation_guidelines.md:184
msgid ""
"a build that selects a different trade-off with the "
"`LIBC_CONF_MATH_OPTIMIZATIONS` option, for example "
"`LIBC_MATH_SKIP_ACCURATE_PASS`, `LIBC_MATH_NO_ERRNO`, or "
"`LIBC_MATH_NO_EXCEPT`. The baremetal and GPU configurations set several of "
"these by default;"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/implementation_guidelines.md:189
msgid ""
"an implementation variant other than the default one (see {ref}"
"`math_function_structure`);"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/implementation_guidelines.md:191
msgid ""
"the GPU-specific math implementations, which have different accuracy bounds "
"and are maintained by the GPU maintainers."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/implementation_guidelines.md:193
msgid ""
"The {doc}`math.h status tables <../headers/math>` record the accuracy of "
"each function, including any that are not correctly rounded yet."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/implementation_guidelines.md:195
msgid ""
"`errno` and floating point exceptions are reported only through "
"`fputil::set_errno_if_required` and `fputil::raise_except_if_required`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/implementation_guidelines.md:200
msgid "Structure"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/implementation_guidelines.md:202
msgid ""
"The implementation lives in a header under `src/__support/math/`, and the "
"entrypoint is a thin wrapper."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/implementation_guidelines.md:204
msgid ""
"A function can have several implementations, each in its own namespace "
"(`double_eval`, `float_eval`, `float_eval_cr_rn`, `integer_eval`), with "
"`LIBC_MATH_<FUNC>_IMPL` selecting the one to use. This is the intended "
"structure for every function, although only some functions have been "
"converted so far. Variants other than the default may make different "
"guarantees."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/implementation_guidelines.md:210
msgid ""
"The functions in `math::static_rounding` take the rounding mode as an "
"argument. They exist for callers that need a fixed rounding mode, such as "
"compilers. They are not the primary implementation of a function. The "
"primary implementation is whichever one is best for the target's "
"configuration."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/implementation_guidelines.md:215
msgid ""
"A pull request may add a function for a single floating point type. It does "
"not need to cover every type at once."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/implementation_guidelines.md:218
msgid "Testing"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/implementation_guidelines.md:220
msgid ""
"The author of a math change runs the unit, smoke, and exhaustive tests for "
"it on their own machine, at least in the default configuration, and "
"preferably before sending the pull request. Reviewers check that this was "
"done. CI covers most of the tests and some configurations, not all of them."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/implementation_guidelines.md:224
msgid "The exhaustive tests take a long time and are not run by `check-libc`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/implementation_guidelines.md:225
msgid ""
"If the tests are hard to run, or hard to get passing, ask the math "
"maintainers for help."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/implementation_guidelines.md:227
msgid ""
"Tests that compare against MPFR run only in overlay mode. Every function "
"also needs a smoke test, which is what runs in full builds and on baremetal "
"and GPU targets."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/implementation_guidelines.md:230
msgid ""
"`libc/test/src/math/exhaustive/` contains hard-to-round inputs from the "
"[CORE-MATH](https://core-math.gitlabpages.inria.fr/) project, as text files "
"(`.wc`) and in a compact binary form (`.bin`). The two forms must stay "
"consistent."
msgstr ""
